1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
A building collapses.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Hundreds are killed in the crush of concrete.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:14,000
One small mechanical malfunction brings down a commercial plane.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Exploding boilers kill thousands on the Mississippi River.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:26,000
A fatal accident in the Alps traps hundreds of people in a smoke-filled road tunnel.

6
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,300
And a mysterious flood shuts down Chicago.

7
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,300
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:58,960
In September 1991, a dredging company replaced the wooden pilings around the Kinsey Street

9
00:00:58,961 --> 00:00:59,900
Bridge in Chicago.

10
00:00:59,901 --> 00:01:08,220
The bilings were supposed to protect the bridge and bridge house from the busy river traffic

11
00:01:08,221 --> 00:01:14,280
But instead they would lead to one of the greatest structural disasters in Chicago history

12
00:01:14,281 --> 00:01:19,180
Seven months later

13
00:01:19,181 --> 00:01:26,180
The first call came into the fire department about two minutes to six in the morning

14
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:32,280
And I heard the fire department respond to water in the basement in the merchandise mart.

15
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:39,740
Approximately 17 other buildings had reported flooding by 6.30.

16
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:43,640
And we're watching a strange story.

17
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:47,000
Flooding reported in several loop buildings this morning.

18
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:55,120
On April 13, 1992, something catastrophic was going on in the commercial section of Chicago, known as the Loop.

19
00:01:56,180 --> 00:02:01,800
Water was flooding into the basements and rapidly rising.

20
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:12,220
The loop is filled with office buildings, big corporations, the Board of Trade, the Mercantile Exchange, city and county government, state government, Marshall Fields.

21
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:20,482
It actually submerged two basements, which is about 40 feet of water.

22
00:02:20,483 --> 00:02:23,400
So, yeah, that was quite astonishing.

23
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:28,400
The Chicago Fire Department immediately began evacuation.

24
00:02:28,401 --> 00:02:33,400
The reason being was that there's transformers in the sub-basements of the buildings.

25
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:39,400
Once the water reaches a certain level in the basements, they're going to short out the transformers,

26
00:02:39,401 --> 00:02:42,400
which will negate power to the buildings.

27
00:02:42,401 --> 00:02:47,400
In other words, you have no elevators, you have no fire protection.

28
00:02:47,401 --> 00:02:52,960
More than a million people were forced to leave the Loop area.

29
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:57,020
The flood made it a ghost town.

30
00:02:57,660 --> 00:02:59,920
The center of Chicago is devoid of people.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,380
It was like a neutron bomb went off.

32
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:06,660
By 10 a.m., several hundred buildings in Chicago had been inundated.

33
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:11,900
Chicago Fire Department trying to determine where these water main breaks are coming from.

34
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:16,320
There was numerous reports of fish in the basements.

35
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,560
And I immediately said, I know where it's coming from.

36
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:23,420
It must be the tunnel.

37
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:28,880
All these older buildings had one thing in common.

38
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:32,840
They were connected to the freight tunnel system and still had open entrances.

39
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,240
This is the freight tunnel.

40
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:45,480
The freight tunnel system is an underground labyrinth of passageways

41
00:03:45,481 --> 00:03:48,660
that runs for 60 miles under the city of Chicago.

42
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,520
We're 40 feet down below the city of Chicago,

43
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:54,580
and you have this amazing network of tunnels down here.

44
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:59,560
In 1898, the privately owned Automatic Telephone Company

45
00:03:59,561 --> 00:04:01,520
got permission to build the tunnels.

46
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:05,260
They planned to use them as a place to run the cables

47
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:07,580
for their new dial telephone system.

48
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:13,000
In the early 1900s, the tunnel project was expanded

49
00:04:13,001 --> 00:04:15,380
to include tracks for a small-gauge railroad.

50
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:20,640
The trains made deliveries to the basements of the stores and other businesses in the loop.

51
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:23,080
They delivered coal for heating.

52
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:24,560
They removed the ashes.

53
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:28,100
They picked up parcels at the large department stores.

54
00:04:28,101 --> 00:04:32,240
The tunnels were dug by hand, 40 feet below Chicago.

55
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:35,980
The rail business reached its zenith in the 1920s,

56
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:40,320
with more than 300 locomotives and 3,000 freight cars in operation.

57
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:51,460
I couldn't stop admiring the engineering vision, the commercial vision associated with it, and the workers who built it.

58
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:56,420
Some of the tunnels were built under the Chicago River.

59
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:01,780
These had to be dug deeper than the underland tunnels to avoid the heavy river traffic.

60
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:06,620
There were about a dozen river crossings.

61
00:05:06,621 --> 00:05:13,700
They were about 60 feet below the surface level or about 20 feet below the river itself.

62
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:19,900
On April 13, 1992, as the water continued to deluge the loop basements,

63
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:28,760
city emergency workers discovered an eddy, or small whirlpool, in the Chicago River at the Kinsey Street Bridge.

64
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:34,820
The bridge was one of the areas where the freight tunnels ran underneath.

65
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:46,120
Knowing that the tunnel was 60 miles long and like 7 1⁄2 foot high and 7 1⁄2 foot wide, we knew we had a problem.

66
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:51,500
Emergency crews established that the tunnel under the bridge had been breached.

67
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:55,700
They immediately tried to stem the flow of water.

68
00:05:55,900 --> 00:06:02,620
We walked over to the river, and there in the river was a barge with fire department personnel on it,

69
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:06,440
throwing mattresses and rocks into this whirlpool.

70
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:10,740
in the river. It is clear that the city cannot live

71
00:06:10,741 --> 00:06:13,940
with a threat like this hanging over our heads.

72
00:06:13,941 --> 00:06:17,220
As the crews worked to stop the flood,

73
00:06:17,221 --> 00:06:20,540
Mayor Richard Daley called in failure analysis experts

74
00:06:20,541 --> 00:06:24,140
to figure out exactly how and when the tunnel was breached.

75
00:06:24,141 --> 00:06:28,300
The first most of the public heard about this event was the day it happened,

76
00:06:28,301 --> 00:06:33,660
April 13th of 1992. In fact the events were set in motion in September

77
00:06:33,661 --> 00:06:34,820
of the prior year.

78
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:39,120
During that time period, a company called

79
00:06:39,121 --> 00:06:41,140
Great Lakes Dredge and Dock Company

80
00:06:41,141 --> 00:06:43,540
had won the contract from the city of Chicago

81
00:06:43,541 --> 00:06:47,100
to replace some pilings that were used

82
00:06:47,101 --> 00:06:50,160
just on the south side of the Kinsey Street Bridge.

83
00:06:50,161 --> 00:06:54,960
This work is routine work and not terribly complicated.

84
00:06:54,961 --> 00:06:58,800
What's required is that the old fender pilings

85
00:06:58,801 --> 00:07:02,120
be extracted and then replaced with new.

86
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:08,880
The pilings were clusters of 37 wooden stakes, each one 50 feet long.

87
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:14,240
The dredging company was contracted to put the new pilings exactly where the old ones were.

88
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:18,960
But when they got to the site, they realized that would be impossible.

89
00:07:20,060 --> 00:07:25,600
There had been a tender house, which is hung on the side of the bridge, that had been moved.

90
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:33,440
And if we were going to put the new pilings where the old pilings had been, we would have destroyed the house.

91
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:41,580
So we asked for and were given permission to move three and a half feet to the south with the new cluster of pilings.

92
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:44,900
The company completed their job in a week and moved on,

93
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:50,960
unaware that they had placed the new pilings three feet closer to the freight tunnel than the old ones.

94
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:55,520
This small move set the wheels in motion for the disaster.

95
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:01,240
When you drive a pile down through a clay soil,

96
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:05,480
the clay has to be moved, has to be displaced for the width of the pile.

97
00:08:05,481 --> 00:08:09,960
And these piles are down at their tips or maybe a foot in diameter or so,

98
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:13,000
and you've got 37 of them being driven down into a tight cluster.

99
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:16,040
So the soil that that's driving into has to be pushed out.

100
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:21,080
Well, that pushing out pushed into the side wall of the freight tunnel and made the wall collapse.

101
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:26,960
That actually all happened back in September of 91, but nobody at the time knew it.

102
00:08:26,961 --> 00:08:35,960
In the 1990s, the freight tunnels were being used to run fiber optics, cable, and other utilities.

103
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:42,240
In January of 1992, a couple of cable installers discovered the breach in the tunnel.

104
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,260
They made a videotape of the hole and gave it to the city.

105
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:48,680
But there was a bureaucratic snag.

106
00:08:49,940 --> 00:08:54,200
In the budget that had been developed for that fiscal year,

107
00:08:54,201 --> 00:08:59,440
the little unit that was responsible for dealing with the tunnels

108
00:08:59,441 --> 00:09:02,100
got moved from one department to another department.

109
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:06,220
And no one really knew who they reported to anymore,

110
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:10,020
so they just put the video aside and didn't tell anyone.

111
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:13,920
Jurisdiction for the tunnels had been moved to the Transportation Department,

112
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:19,020
and they didn't become aware of the video and the tunnel breach until March.

113
00:09:19,021 --> 00:09:26,120
Since they knew the hole had already been there for at least two months, no one felt it was an emergency.

114
00:09:27,180 --> 00:09:30,220
But the department did seek estimates for the repair of the breach.

115
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:35,100
They finally awarded a contractor $7,000 to fix the tunnel.

116
00:09:36,860 --> 00:09:39,120
Unfortunately, they waited too long.

117
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:48,800
This was a serious problem that required immediate repairs and it should have been expedited.

118
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:54,020
Local businesses dealt with the disaster the best they could.

119
00:09:54,021 --> 00:09:59,020
We were trying to get rid of all of the food that we possibly could

120
00:09:59,021 --> 00:10:04,020
because everything was shut down, the freezers, the ovens, and everything else.

121
00:10:04,021 --> 00:10:06,020
We were worried about all the people who had bought items

122
00:10:06,021 --> 00:10:08,020
and were going to have them shipped to them

123
00:10:08,021 --> 00:10:11,020
because all those items were down in our second basement,

124
00:10:11,021 --> 00:10:13,020
which was underwater at that point.

125
00:10:13,021 --> 00:10:18,020
Chicago was officially declared a disaster area on April 15th

126
00:10:18,021 --> 00:10:24,700
and FEMA, the Federal Emergency Management Agency, began distributing funds for the repair and cleanup process.

127
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:33,020
They called up the Army Corps of Engineers and gave them the mission of finding a way to quickly plug up the hole in the tunnel

128
00:10:33,021 --> 00:10:35,860
and temporarily contain the water flow.

129
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:40,920
Now the freight tunnel in this location actually goes in two directions.

130
00:10:41,400 --> 00:10:46,380
It goes east on Kenzie Street, and it also goes west on Kenzie Street in two directions.

131
00:10:46,381 --> 00:10:53,380
So to plug the leak, we sank three shafts on each side of the river, dropped in sandbags,

132
00:10:53,381 --> 00:10:59,380
and then surcharged it all the way to the top of the street with stone to temporarily stop the flow.

133
00:10:59,381 --> 00:11:06,380
Twenty feet away from there, we drove some other shafts on Kenzie Street, had divers go down into the tunnel,

134
00:11:06,381 --> 00:11:09,380
chipped away to debris at the bottom of the tunnel.

135
00:11:09,381 --> 00:11:15,380
In that case, we were able to pour concrete from the bottom of the tunnel up all the way to the top of the shaft.

136
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:22,880
Once the flow was stopped, the dewatering phase could begin.

137
00:11:22,881 --> 00:11:29,500
More than 1.5 million tons of water had flooded the loop.

138
00:11:29,501 --> 00:11:34,120
As the Army Corps of Engineers pumped the water out of the loop, the mayor pulled together

139
00:11:34,121 --> 00:11:37,660
a team to permanently secure the tunnels.

140
00:11:37,661 --> 00:11:46,340
The city hired engineers and there was a great effort put out and a teamwork thing

141
00:11:46,341 --> 00:11:49,600
to prevent this thing from happening.

142
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:52,980
Can we anticipate everything?

143
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:54,040
No.

144
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:55,580
You do the best you can,

145
00:11:55,660 --> 00:11:57,620
and I think that was a great example

146
00:11:57,621 --> 00:12:00,700
of how engineers, contractors,

147
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:05,000
and the city officials, and that all work together.

148
00:12:06,740 --> 00:12:09,780
What we did is we've done bulkheaded

149
00:12:09,781 --> 00:12:11,600
every crossing at the river,

150
00:12:11,601 --> 00:12:14,440
so both sides of every crossing have this.

151
00:12:16,340 --> 00:12:22,700
And then the other thing is there's sensors in there that trigger once the water level reaches a certain height,

152
00:12:22,701 --> 00:12:28,380
and we get up about a foot or two, it would trigger the alarm and let us know if there's water in the bulkhead.

153
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:35,180
The people of Chicago took the whole event in stride.

154
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:42,200
We knew we had a big problem on our hands, and we worked day and night until the problem was solved.

155
00:12:42,201 --> 00:12:46,700
That's what Chicago, that's how Chicago works, the city that works.

156
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:48,380
And it worked.

157
00:12:50,480 --> 00:12:54,580
The ultimate cost of the Chicago flood reached the $1 billion mark.

158
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:58,280
But in one way, the city was lucky.

159
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:03,480
There were no fatalities, and in fact, no injuries were reported.

160
00:13:03,481 --> 00:13:12,320
Approximately 396 million gallons of water poured into the Chicago Loop during the flood.

161
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:16,660
Enough to fill all 110 floors of the Sears Tower.

162
00:13:17,220 --> 00:13:19,880
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

163
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:25,160
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

164
00:13:25,161 --> 00:13:37,420
On March 27, 1981, the five-story Harbor Cay condominium in Cocoa Beach, Florida, collapsed

165
00:13:37,421 --> 00:13:38,860
while it was still under construction.

166
00:13:38,861 --> 00:13:41,700
It literally pancakes.

167
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:50,140
The disastrous accident killed 11 people and injured 23 others.

168
00:13:50,141 --> 00:14:01,740
What you see is columns sticking out, sticking up, and the slabs came down, stripping themselves around the columns.

169
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:11,280
The design of the beach condominium was a simple scheme that was often used in office and residential construction.

170
00:14:11,281 --> 00:14:21,240
Reinforced concrete columns, flat concrete slabs between those columns, rectangular shape.

171
00:14:21,241 --> 00:14:24,820
You construct floor after floor.

172
00:14:24,821 --> 00:14:28,620
Because the accident took place while the building was under construction, it came under

173
00:14:28,621 --> 00:14:35,400
the jurisdiction of the federal government's Occupational Safety and Health Administration.

174
00:14:35,401 --> 00:14:39,820
OSHA immediately brought in investigators from the National Institute of Standards and

175
00:14:39,821 --> 00:14:40,820
Technology.

176
00:14:40,821 --> 00:14:44,260
to determine what caused the disaster at the Cocoa Beach site.

177
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:49,000
Investigating a failure is very much like investigating a crime.

178
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:51,160
You have to sift through a lot of evidence.

179
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,100
Their initial on-site inspection gave the investigators a general idea of what might have happened.

180
00:14:58,580 --> 00:15:04,680
When we looked at the evidence, we saw that a lot of the columns remained vertical,

181
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:10,340
and this told us that the floor slab had separated from the columns,

182
00:15:10,341 --> 00:15:12,880
in what we call a punching shear failure.

183
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:18,100
A punching shear failure happens when the concrete floor slab cracks

184
00:15:18,101 --> 00:15:20,320
and breaks away from its column connection.

185
00:15:24,280 --> 00:15:28,300
It's as though you're poking the column through the floor slab

186
00:15:28,301 --> 00:15:30,280
like a pencil through a piece of paper.

187
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:33,340
The resistance to that penetration

188
00:15:33,341 --> 00:15:36,820
is a function of the strength of the concrete in the slab.

189
00:15:36,821 --> 00:15:44,520
And it's also a function of the thickness of the slab and where the reinforcing steel is placed in the slab.

190
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:49,200
When they analyzed the concrete used in the construction at Harbor K,

191
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:55,680
the investigators discovered that the steel reinforcing bars in the fifth floor slab were placed incorrectly.

192
00:15:56,500 --> 00:16:00,640
The location of the rebar in the concrete is important.

193
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:04,980
Sometimes you need the rebar right under the top surface of the slab.

194
00:16:04,981 --> 00:16:09,140
Sometimes you need it near the bottom surface of the slab.

195
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:16,140
Before the concrete was poured, the pieces of rebar were set on steel chairs to hold them in position.

196
00:16:17,180 --> 00:16:20,180
But in this case, the chairs were too short.

197
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:23,920
The rebar should have been closer to the top of the slab

198
00:16:23,921 --> 00:16:28,080
to give it enough depth and strength to resist the pressure of the column.

199
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:33,940
Near the column, the tension forces are near the top of the slab.

200
00:16:33,941 --> 00:16:39,540
Therefore, you want the reinforcing bars as close to the top surface as possible.

201
00:16:39,541 --> 00:16:44,540
It was actually one inch deeper in the floor slab than it should have been.

202
00:16:44,541 --> 00:16:51,740
As a result of this reduced height, it reduced the resistance of the floor slab to this punching shear failure.

203
00:16:51,741 --> 00:16:57,540
It could have been a mistake by the people who supplied the material,

204
00:16:57,541 --> 00:17:02,140
or somebody picked up the wrong pieces and put it on the truck and delivered.

205
00:17:02,141 --> 00:17:02,920
We don't know.

206
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:08,300
But once we have determined that from measuring the height on the fifth floor,

207
00:17:08,660 --> 00:17:12,700
that was a time we said, aha, we think we know something.

208
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:17,700
Construction workers noticed cracks near the columns on several floors.

209
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:23,260
They notified the engineering designer, who came to the site on March 10, 1981,

210
00:17:24,020 --> 00:17:26,640
just one week before the collapse.

211
00:17:26,641 --> 00:17:35,260
the designer came looked at it and concluded everything is hunky-dory in fact what those

212
00:17:35,261 --> 00:17:42,960
cracks were telling us is that there was a distress at the connection between the slab and the column

213
00:17:42,961 --> 00:17:56,961
14 years after the Harbor Cay disaster on June 29 1995 the Sampung department store in Seoul

214
00:17:58,740 --> 00:18:06,660
South Korea collapsed the disaster occurred during peak shopping hours 502 people were

215
00:18:06,661 --> 00:18:11,460
killed and more than 900 others suffered from grave injuries.

216
00:18:11,461 --> 00:18:13,900
The entire city of Seoul was in

217
00:18:13,901 --> 00:18:20,757
shock. Similarities to the Cocoa Beach collapse in 1981 were numerous.

218
00:18:20,758 --> 00:18:22,520
Again, flat slabs,

219
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:29,820
reinforced concrete columns, most likely sheer punch. The store was built in 1989,

220
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:34,880
and that same year, the owners decided to add another floor to the four-story building.

221
00:18:34,881 --> 00:18:38,907
The engineer of record said that cannot be done.

222
00:18:38,908 --> 00:18:47,380
We are dealing with a structure which was designed over a landfill and we should not add this weight.

223
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:52,340
The owner hired another company which said yes sir, we are going to do it.

224
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:56,620
The fifth floor was built and the building stood for five years.

225
00:18:57,220 --> 00:19:02,540
The collapse was triggered when 10 tons of air conditioning units were added to the roof in 1995.

226
00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:08,520
And that created stresses on the top of the structure, which was already overstressed.

227
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:16,480
Like the Harbor K structure, cracks in the concrete were brought to the Sampung store owners' attention just days before the collapse.

228
00:19:17,560 --> 00:19:26,500
But they ignored the warning signs and refused to evacuate the shoppers and store personnel, a decision that resulted in the high number of deaths and injuries.

229
00:19:26,501 --> 00:19:33,760
though they were in different parts of the world and collapsed 14 years apart

230
00:19:33,761 --> 00:19:38,920
the harbor cay and sampung disasters were both the result of a punching

231
00:19:38,921 --> 00:19:41,460
sheer failure that could have been prevented

232
00:19:41,461 --> 00:19:51,500
in korea the store owners joon lee and his son han song lee were both found guilty of

233
00:19:51,501 --> 00:19:56,100
negligence and bribery and sentenced to prison for seven to ten years.

234
00:19:58,100 --> 00:20:03,540
The state of Florida strengthened its construction safety laws after the disaster in 1981,

235
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:10,200
requiring more scrutiny of construction plans and more on-site inspections by engineers.

236
00:20:13,120 --> 00:20:16,580
One cubic yard of concrete weighs over one and a half tons,

237
00:20:16,581 --> 00:20:20,900
but requires only 50 pounds of steel to reinforce it as flooring.

238
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:25,500
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

239
00:20:27,500 --> 00:20:31,500
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

240
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:46,500
At 9 a.m., August 31, 1988, a Delta Boeing 727 airplane, like this one, was cleared for takeoff from Dallas-Fort Worth Airport.

241
00:20:46,501 --> 00:20:55,620
airport the plane bound for Salt Lake City taxied down the runway the pilot

242
00:20:55,621 --> 00:21:01,740
accelerated lifting the nose of the airplane but he never achieved liftoff

243
00:21:01,741 --> 00:21:07,800
the right wing of the 727 hit the ground the plane began to wobble out of

244
00:21:07,801 --> 00:21:17,640
control and then it crashed. Eyewitnesses say it looked like a cracked egg.

245
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:26,755
I know people were in danger.

246
00:21:26,756 --> 00:21:30,840
They needed us and the main thing that I was thinking is

247
00:21:30,841 --> 00:21:33,400
is getting there as fast as I could.

248
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:39,400
The airport fire department arrived on the scene within two minutes.

249
00:21:39,780 --> 00:21:40,480
It was smoking.

250
00:21:40,860 --> 00:21:45,380
As I got closer, I could see the fire towering between the smoke.

251
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:49,640
They put foam and water on the largest volume of the fire

252
00:21:49,641 --> 00:21:54,560
and quickly secured that fuselage area because that's where our passengers are at.

253
00:21:54,561 --> 00:22:05,020
Of the 147 people on board, one flight attendant and 13 passengers died,

254
00:22:05,640 --> 00:22:10,560
and 16 were seriously injured from the fire and smoke that consumed the mangled airplane.

255
00:22:11,220 --> 00:22:13,940
We're very fortunate that as many people survived as they did.

256
00:22:14,040 --> 00:22:17,300
I've seen accidents not any worse than this where everyone died.

257
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:23,980
A team of investigators from the National Transportation Safety Board was immediately assembled

258
00:22:23,981 --> 00:22:26,240
and dispatched to the site of the crash.

259
00:22:27,820 --> 00:22:32,560
They would find that mechanical failure and human error had combined in a deadly fashion.

260
00:22:33,340 --> 00:22:35,560
I was with the team and the board member on scene.

261
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:38,240
People sometimes think of us as puzzle solvers.

262
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:39,380
That's basically what it is.

263
00:22:39,740 --> 00:22:42,080
You collect facts, you analyze those facts.

264
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:44,080
Where do those facts lead you?

265
00:22:44,180 --> 00:22:47,960
They lead you to, in this case, a cause of an accident, and then you go from there.

266
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:51,700
One of the first things the investigators discovered

267
00:22:51,701 --> 00:22:56,080
was that the flaps on the airplane's wings were in the retracted up position.

268
00:22:56,860 --> 00:23:00,080
They should have been set down at a 15-degree angle

269
00:23:00,081 --> 00:23:02,480
to allow the plane enough lift for takeoff.

270
00:23:02,940 --> 00:23:06,280
One may question why could three people in a cockpit

271
00:23:06,281 --> 00:23:08,920
allow the plane to take off with all of them knowing

272
00:23:08,921 --> 00:23:10,300
that it should have been at 15 degrees

273
00:23:10,301 --> 00:23:12,100
and take off with the flaps at zero degrees.

274
00:23:12,580 --> 00:23:14,040
And there are reasons that that occurred.

275
00:23:15,980 --> 00:23:20,620
The NTSB team learned that after Flight 1141 had pulled out from the gate

276
00:23:20,621 --> 00:23:23,120
and the crew was preparing the takeoff checklist.

277
00:23:24,140 --> 00:23:27,460
Air traffic control delayed the flight for an indefinite period of time.

278
00:23:29,020 --> 00:23:32,120
At this point, the crew made a crucial error in judgment.

279
00:23:33,380 --> 00:23:35,600
They didn't finish going through the checklist.

280
00:23:37,420 --> 00:23:40,280
Instead, while they waited for clearance to take off,

281
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:44,780
the three-man cockpit crew engaged in casual conversation with a flight attendant.

282
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:50,500
According to the FAA, this is a violation of the sterile cockpit rule.

283
00:23:50,620 --> 00:23:54,560
Sterile means there's no extraneous conversations going on.

284
00:23:54,620 --> 00:23:59,100
You're not talking about baseball scores or who the Cowboys are playing next week

285
00:23:59,101 --> 00:24:01,720
or any other kind of things that people always talk about.

286
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:06,320
Stick to flying the airplane.

287
00:24:06,480 --> 00:24:09,080
That's all you should be talking about from the time you leave the gate

288
00:24:09,081 --> 00:24:10,440
until you get over 10,000 feet.

289
00:24:11,280 --> 00:24:13,880
There were four planes ahead of Flight 1141,

290
00:24:14,520 --> 00:24:18,040
but air traffic control decided to give them permission to go around the others

291
00:24:18,041 --> 00:24:20,420
and get in position for immediate takeoff.

292
00:24:20,620 --> 00:24:27,520
And you could tell by listening to the cockpit voice recorder that there was some, I won't say pressure,

293
00:24:27,680 --> 00:24:31,620
but they were speeding up, if you will, the process of finishing the checklist.

294
00:24:33,320 --> 00:24:39,040
When it came to verifying the position of the wing flaps, the response was 15-15 green,

295
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:44,340
which meant both wings were set properly to 15 degrees and ready for takeoff.

296
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:48,340
Air off, flap. 15-15 green line, flight control.

297
00:24:48,341 --> 00:24:55,520
In our opinion, we can't answer why the individual said the 1515 green light

298
00:24:55,521 --> 00:24:59,380
when we are positive that it was set at zero.

299
00:25:01,460 --> 00:25:03,880
But even with all of these human errors,

300
00:25:04,520 --> 00:25:10,100
the 727 was equipped with a mechanical fail-safe device called the takeoff warning system.

301
00:25:10,101 --> 00:25:13,760
As soon as the engine power got advanced in the takeoff warning system,

302
00:25:14,060 --> 00:25:15,780
there'd been all sorts of warning horns going off saying,

303
00:25:15,781 --> 00:25:18,340
hey, dummy, put the flaps down. We're not ready to take off yet.

304
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:24,380
But the warning horn on Delta Flight 1141 didn't go off.

305
00:25:25,080 --> 00:25:28,040
Aviation, the old expression is hours and hours of boredom

306
00:25:28,041 --> 00:25:30,220
punctuated by a few moments of stark terror.

307
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:34,220
And your job is to prepare yourself for these few moments of stark terror.

308
00:25:36,380 --> 00:25:39,860
There are two theories that could explain the takeoff warning system failure.

309
00:25:41,660 --> 00:25:44,900
One is that the device was assembled improperly at the factory.

310
00:25:45,780 --> 00:25:49,040
The other suggests a problem in the airplane itself.

311
00:25:49,880 --> 00:25:53,760
There was a minor electrical system malfunction and a circuit breaker that was out

312
00:25:53,761 --> 00:25:56,140
that the flight engineer had detected,

313
00:25:56,620 --> 00:25:58,600
but he didn't think it was going to be a major problem,

314
00:25:59,040 --> 00:26:03,260
and he didn't calculate that that would have disabled to take off warning horns.

315
00:26:04,760 --> 00:26:10,420
Since the crash of Flight 1141, the sterile cockpit rule is more rigorously observed.

316
00:26:10,421 --> 00:26:20,200
I think all of us in aviation, we're more cognizant of the fact that it's easy to overlook things.

317
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:23,140
And so you don't need any distractions.

318
00:26:25,980 --> 00:26:29,440
Flight crews are more diligent about performing their pre-flight checks.

319
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:33,580
One of the things that I think more people are doing is they take the runway now,

320
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:36,460
is to do this additional last-minute check.

321
00:26:36,461 --> 00:26:40,400
Even though 30 minutes earlier they might have checked, the flaps were down.

322
00:26:40,740 --> 00:26:42,940
Check again. Check again. Check again.

323
00:26:44,760 --> 00:26:50,280
And manufacturers have improved the takeoff warning systems throughout the airline industry.

324
00:26:54,180 --> 00:26:58,220
The 727 cockpit voice recorder can record up to 30 minutes

325
00:26:58,221 --> 00:27:02,180
and withstand temperatures of over 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

326
00:27:02,181 --> 00:27:07,060
engineering disasters will return on modern marvels

327
00:27:07,061 --> 00:27:12,740
we now return to engineering disasters on modern marvels

328
00:27:12,741 --> 00:27:22,820
suddenly to be awakened at 2 a.m with this huge explosion

329
00:27:22,821 --> 00:27:30,760
the decks collapsing having no earthly idea where the safety of dry land was

330
00:27:30,761 --> 00:27:34,040
no life vests, no lifeboats.

331
00:27:36,200 --> 00:27:40,120
It was the largest maritime disaster in history.

332
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:42,700
Larger than the Titanic.

333
00:27:45,220 --> 00:27:47,920
Much larger in loss of life.

334
00:27:50,980 --> 00:27:55,380
What I am always amazed by is the fact that so many soldiers died.

335
00:27:56,440 --> 00:27:59,320
So many men who fought for their country.

336
00:27:59,321 --> 00:28:04,060
and this event has been entirely forgotten by history.

337
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:16,580
On April 24, 1865, the steamboat Sultana left Vicksburg, Mississippi,

338
00:28:16,900 --> 00:28:18,520
heading north up the Mississippi River.

339
00:28:20,040 --> 00:28:24,020
It was packed with approximately 2,300 passengers and crew,

340
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:27,660
six times the usual number for such a paddlewheel vessel.

341
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:30,320
The Civil War was just ending,

342
00:28:30,900 --> 00:28:33,440
and most of the travelers on board were Union soldiers

343
00:28:33,441 --> 00:28:36,460
who had recently been released from southern prison camps.

344
00:28:37,080 --> 00:28:39,600
There was barely enough room to lie down.

345
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,580
There was one cook stove with that entire boat.

346
00:28:43,420 --> 00:28:46,300
So a lot of the men had to eat their rations raw.

347
00:28:47,340 --> 00:28:48,440
But, you know, it didn't matter.

348
00:28:49,260 --> 00:28:50,420
They were on their way home.

349
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:55,020
On April 27th,

350
00:28:55,021 --> 00:28:57,500
three of the Sultana's boilers exploded.

351
00:28:57,660 --> 00:29:03,060
the force of the blast blew scalding steam and flames throughout the wooden boat.

352
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:08,380
Those who weren't killed instantly jumped into the river,

353
00:29:08,760 --> 00:29:11,680
but most of them drowned before they ever made it to shore.

354
00:29:13,280 --> 00:29:17,940
The state of boiler technology at that time was pretty crude and pretty rudimentary.

355
00:29:19,300 --> 00:29:23,280
The Sultana used elder boilers, manufactured in England.

356
00:29:23,281 --> 00:29:28,240
They were 18 feet long and 4 feet in diameter

357
00:29:28,241 --> 00:29:33,540
Each of the boilers had 24 flues

358
00:29:33,541 --> 00:29:36,540
Open cylinders that ran from the front to the back

359
00:29:36,541 --> 00:29:38,520
Which were 5 inches in diameter

360
00:29:38,521 --> 00:29:41,880
And these boilers would have been sitting side by side

361
00:29:41,881 --> 00:29:47,920
And they would have been encased in a protective sheeting

362
00:29:47,921 --> 00:29:51,220
Underneath were 6 foot long coal furnaces

363
00:29:51,221 --> 00:29:53,920
that generated heat for the water in the boilers,

364
00:29:54,280 --> 00:29:56,100
but only for the forward portion.

365
00:29:57,020 --> 00:30:01,180
The 24 flues carried hot air to the back to heat the rest of the water.

366
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:04,600
So what happens is the fire heats up the air,

367
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,800
and the air by a draft is pulled under the boilers,

368
00:30:08,801 --> 00:30:12,520
then through these flues, and then up the smokestacks.

369
00:30:14,580 --> 00:30:17,880
The boiling water became steam, which powered the boat.

370
00:30:21,220 --> 00:30:28,860
So that's a really basic way that the boilers would have worked on the Sultana or on any steamboat at that time.

371
00:30:30,720 --> 00:30:36,580
At the time of the Sultana disaster, steamboats were a common mode of transportation on the Mississippi River.

372
00:30:37,640 --> 00:30:41,580
The mid to late 1800s was what we called the golden age of steamboats.

373
00:30:41,760 --> 00:30:43,920
This was the superhighway of that time period.

374
00:30:43,921 --> 00:30:50,880
So you had literally hundreds, thousands of steamboats plying the waters, running short, little ten-mile jaunts from one city to the next.

375
00:30:50,881 --> 00:30:52,600
the river was filled with those.

376
00:30:53,440 --> 00:30:56,320
And when you figure that many steamboats that were traveling the rivers,

377
00:30:56,380 --> 00:30:58,920
in the 1800s, not one of them is left.

378
00:30:59,580 --> 00:31:01,580
They only lasted about three to five years.

379
00:31:02,820 --> 00:31:06,500
Many of the steamboats on the Mississippi were destroyed by boiler explosions.

380
00:31:07,200 --> 00:31:09,500
There wasn't much regulations on those boilers,

381
00:31:10,060 --> 00:31:14,420
and it was one of the real serious things to have, of course, a boiler explosion,

382
00:31:14,421 --> 00:31:19,580
because subsequently, if that happened, you had not only scalding water and shrapnel,

383
00:31:19,581 --> 00:31:24,080
You had splinters of woods and always a fire because it also blowed the furnace apart.

384
00:31:24,780 --> 00:31:28,060
The Sultana was only two years old in 1865,

385
00:31:28,360 --> 00:31:31,240
but all four of her boilers were in need of maintenance.

386
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:33,900
They had to be fixed a number of times,

387
00:31:34,620 --> 00:31:39,840
and even the trip when the soldiers were being placed on board the Sultana at Vicksburg,

388
00:31:40,200 --> 00:31:43,920
the boilers were being repaired because one of the middle left-hand boilers

389
00:31:43,921 --> 00:31:46,060
had sprung a leak down near the furnace.

390
00:31:49,580 --> 00:31:54,780
The captain of the Sultana, James Cass Mason, was also one of the ship's owners.

391
00:31:55,560 --> 00:31:59,720
He routinely overtaxed the boilers, trying to make his steamboat run faster.

392
00:32:00,280 --> 00:32:02,960
He's like what I like to call a leadfoot nowadays.

393
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:05,160
He loved the races, his steamboat.

394
00:32:05,580 --> 00:32:06,800
And he didn't have a lot of money.

395
00:32:06,900 --> 00:32:07,880
He owed a lot of people.

396
00:32:08,180 --> 00:32:10,360
He let his boilers go into bad repair.

397
00:32:11,540 --> 00:32:15,100
When the Union Army was choosing a boat to carry soldiers back home,

398
00:32:15,780 --> 00:32:20,860
Cass Mason bribed one of the officers to let him take more than 1,300 men on board the Sultana,

399
00:32:21,640 --> 00:32:23,540
knowing it could be a safety hazard.

400
00:32:24,500 --> 00:32:30,340
The military was paying $5 per enlisted man, $10 per officer, and that was a lot of money.

401
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:34,440
And Mason was a man that was in financial trouble. He needed the money.

402
00:32:35,520 --> 00:32:40,480
The trip up the Mississippi was difficult for the Sultana because the river was at a flood stage,

403
00:32:40,920 --> 00:32:42,380
so the current was strong.

404
00:32:42,381 --> 00:32:48,140
To make headway, the ship had to crisscross from one side of the river to the other.

405
00:32:48,560 --> 00:32:52,140
It was going sort of perpendicular to the river and getting hit in the side

406
00:32:52,141 --> 00:32:55,400
with this strong flood current tilting the boat.

407
00:32:56,020 --> 00:32:58,320
As the Sultana tilted back and forth,

408
00:32:58,720 --> 00:33:01,420
the water in the upper boilers flowed into the lower ones.

409
00:33:03,140 --> 00:33:06,100
This exposed the metal in the upper boilers to the fire

410
00:33:06,101 --> 00:33:08,900
without any water to protect them from getting too hot.

411
00:33:08,901 --> 00:33:15,280
And as this boat now tilts back to an even keel and the water flows back to that boiler,

412
00:33:15,480 --> 00:33:20,640
it's going to touch that red-hot metal and it is suddenly going to turn into excess steam.

413
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:25,620
To keep up the Sultana's speed against the strong current,

414
00:33:26,200 --> 00:33:31,140
the chief engineer increased the steam pressure by tampering with the safety valves on the boilers.

415
00:33:32,380 --> 00:33:36,280
What happened is a lot of engineers would tie down the safety valve

416
00:33:36,281 --> 00:33:40,160
or tie weights onto the safety valve to weigh it down

417
00:33:40,161 --> 00:33:44,880
so when it hit 125, 130, 135, it wouldn't blow.

418
00:33:45,540 --> 00:33:49,640
The hot metal, high pressure, and faulty boilers combined

419
00:33:49,641 --> 00:33:51,460
to cause the deadly explosion.

420
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:54,200
There was probably weaknesses all throughout the boiler,

421
00:33:54,340 --> 00:33:58,520
and all steam needs is one little opening and one little bad spot,

422
00:33:58,521 --> 00:34:00,900
and if the pressure increases, it's gone.

423
00:34:00,901 --> 00:34:08,960
My theory is that the one exploded, it probably sent shards of itself into the opposite two on the other sides.

424
00:34:09,440 --> 00:34:12,100
And then once those are punctured, then they blew up.

425
00:34:21,460 --> 00:34:25,420
Today, there are still a handful of paddle boats cruising the Mississippi River.

426
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:28,620
And they still use steam as their source of power.

427
00:34:28,621 --> 00:34:34,240
Our boilers are fully automated, so we have a lot of alarms and a lot of indicators,

428
00:34:34,960 --> 00:34:38,900
so engineers can trace and see what's going on at all times.

429
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:46,980
We watch all parameters, boiler parameters, steam engine parameters, electrical parameters, all equipment.

430
00:34:48,360 --> 00:34:52,380
We have navigational aids. We have all the safety equipment on board.

431
00:34:52,880 --> 00:34:56,680
It isn't likely we're going to have something like happen with the Sultana.

432
00:34:58,620 --> 00:35:04,220
News of the Sultana disaster didn't make the headlines.

433
00:35:05,360 --> 00:35:08,420
Other major events were taking place in April 1865.

434
00:35:09,880 --> 00:35:11,720
Lincoln had just been assassinated.

435
00:35:12,160 --> 00:35:15,500
The war had ended at Appomattox a few weeks before.

436
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:23,060
And the stories about the Sultana, if they were even in the paper, was on the fourth and the fifth page.

437
00:35:23,360 --> 00:35:27,020
Even books about the end of the Civil War don't mention the Sultana.

438
00:35:27,021 --> 00:35:33,040
And I think it's a travesty to these men's memories and their actions for their country that they've been totally forgotten.

439
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:45,740
As tragic as it is, there were a lot of new regulations put up, changed after the fact to help prevent things like this from happening again.

440
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:52,920
As a direct result of the Sultana tragedy, the first boiler inspection and insurance company was formed.

441
00:35:52,921 --> 00:35:59,845
You had to go to school now to become a boiler inspector, and it wasn't as easy to bribe them.

442
00:35:59,846 --> 00:36:01,540
That was another thing.

443
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:15,160
At the National Cemetery in Memphis, Tennessee, the gravestones stand as a permanent reminder of the soldiers that lived through the war, but lost their lives in the

444
00:36:15,161 --> 00:36:16,320
Sultana disaster.

445
00:36:22,920 --> 00:36:34,860
The Sultana had more casualties than all other steamship explosions put together.

446
00:36:34,861 --> 00:36:42,000
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

447
00:36:42,001 --> 00:36:48,160
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

448
00:36:48,161 --> 00:36:55,160
in three European Alpine road tunnels claimed 62 lives between 1999 and 2001.

449
00:36:56,260 --> 00:36:59,060
The Goddard Road Tunnel in Switzerland was one of them.

450
00:37:02,820 --> 00:37:09,600
On October 24, 2001, in the 10 and a half mile long two-way road tunnel, a truck driver

451
00:37:09,601 --> 00:37:15,240
swerved into oncoming traffic, collided with another truck, and crashed into the tunnel

452
00:37:15,241 --> 00:37:15,941
wall.

453
00:37:15,942 --> 00:37:27,120
At the end we had the result that he was drunken and I think this kind of drivers

454
00:37:27,121 --> 00:37:29,492
should not be on our road system.

455
00:37:29,493 --> 00:37:33,660
Diesel fuel spilled out of the truck's gas tank and caught

456
00:37:33,661 --> 00:37:39,026
fire. The truck was loaded with several tons of tires that also ignited.

457
00:37:39,027 --> 00:37:40,740
The fire developed very

458
00:37:40,741 --> 00:37:46,180
quickly in one two minutes the fire was open fire tunnel traffic came to a

459
00:37:46,181 --> 00:37:52,200
standstill the two burning trucks blocked both lanes 11 people died but

460
00:37:52,201 --> 00:37:55,920
they didn't perish in the raging fire all of the victims died from smoke

461
00:37:55,921 --> 00:38:03,060
inhalation and the fact is known that in a tunnel the smoke is the real risk not

462
00:38:03,061 --> 00:38:09,540
the fire and not all the people were aware of this danger

463
00:38:09,541 --> 00:38:13,780
The ventilation system in the tunnel was able to suck some of the deadly smoke out,

464
00:38:14,340 --> 00:38:16,640
but most of it spread to the northern end of the tunnel,

465
00:38:17,160 --> 00:38:19,080
completely enveloping the trapped vehicles.

466
00:38:19,280 --> 00:38:22,940
You know, the fire brigade was immediately alarmed.

467
00:38:24,300 --> 00:38:27,060
Attention. This is a police announcement.

468
00:38:27,720 --> 00:38:29,960
A vehicle is on fire in the tunnel.

469
00:38:30,700 --> 00:38:32,380
Passage is partially blocked.

470
00:38:32,381 --> 00:38:36,460
The police aired announcements over the radio to instruct people trapped in the tunnel

471
00:38:36,461 --> 00:38:40,180
to get out of their cars and find one of the emergency escape shelters.

472
00:38:40,820 --> 00:38:44,900
Follow the emergency traffic signals to the nearest escape shelter.

473
00:38:45,480 --> 00:38:51,580
In the Götet Road Tunnel we have these shelters, I say the rescue boats.

474
00:38:52,120 --> 00:38:53,520
You will be safe in the shelter.

475
00:38:54,240 --> 00:38:56,680
The tunnel houses 64 shelters.

476
00:38:57,620 --> 00:39:01,320
These airtight rooms are equipped with first aid kits, fire extinguishers,

477
00:39:01,680 --> 00:39:03,380
and instructions on what to do next.

478
00:39:06,460 --> 00:39:11,500
the shelters are connected to a safety tunnel a narrower passageway that runs parallel to the

479
00:39:11,501 --> 00:39:17,340
road tunnel with exits on both ends but not everyone heard the radio announcement on october

480
00:39:17,341 --> 00:39:23,980
24 2001 and with all the thick black smoke they couldn't see the signs posted throughout the tunnel

481
00:39:24,780 --> 00:39:33,020
five were test in their car i don't know if the radio was on to listen what the announcement is

482
00:39:33,021 --> 00:39:34,043
of the police.

483
00:39:34,044 --> 00:39:40,180
And if you follow these announcements, then you have a good chance with our shelters

484
00:39:40,181 --> 00:39:41,220
to survive.

485
00:39:43,320 --> 00:39:49,000
The smoke couldn't penetrate the escape shelters, but the investigation into the disaster confirmed

486
00:39:49,001 --> 00:39:53,720
that the ventilation system in the tunnel itself was not sufficient to handle the smoke

487
00:39:53,721 --> 00:39:56,340
generated from a fire of such great magnitude.

488
00:39:56,341 --> 00:40:10,341
So the 2,000 meters were fully filled up with smoke in direction north because of this old ventilation system.

489
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:16,740
Ironically, at the time of the tragedy, the Goddard Tunnel had already begun updating its ventilation system.

490
00:40:17,400 --> 00:40:23,940
This system was in construction when the fire occurred, but it was not yet operating.

491
00:40:24,580 --> 00:40:28,260
This was another unhappy circumstances that one has to deplore.

492
00:40:29,660 --> 00:40:33,520
The ventilation system in the Goddard Tunnel is a transversal system.

493
00:40:34,340 --> 00:40:39,220
It consists of four large ducts, which are connected to 22 fans throughout the tunnel.

494
00:40:40,380 --> 00:40:46,080
The fans blow fresh air into the tunnel and extract the foul air out through six ventilation stations.

495
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:53,500
This is the system which is installed in most of the long alpine tunnels.

496
00:40:53,940 --> 00:40:58,260
because originally it was considered to be the safest.

497
00:40:59,820 --> 00:41:06,240
After the fire in 2001, motorized dampers were added to the transversal ventilation system.

498
00:41:07,940 --> 00:41:13,680
Engineers cut 178 squares out of the concrete ceiling of the tunnel, 100 yards apart.

499
00:41:14,440 --> 00:41:17,420
Then they installed the dampers in each of the squares.

500
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:21,320
The damper blades are opened and closed by remote control.

501
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:28,920
The dampers pull the smoke or foul air from a specific localized area of the tunnel into the ventilation extraction ducts.

502
00:41:29,360 --> 00:41:39,220
We have the possibility to open dampers in the area where the smoke is and to get it better away from the traffic area.

503
00:41:41,280 --> 00:41:48,020
In case of a fire, the tunnel control center can quickly open the dampers at the site and close all the others.

504
00:41:48,021 --> 00:41:55,020
The smoke is extracted and then released out of the tunnel through smokestacks.

505
00:41:59,020 --> 00:42:06,020
Since the disaster, fire and rescue units are stationed at both entrances of the Goddard Road Tunnel.

506
00:42:08,020 --> 00:42:13,020
And they have two new vehicles, which have several high-tech features.

507
00:42:13,021 --> 00:42:17,980
Both vehicles are equipped with infrared heat sensing cameras,

508
00:42:18,120 --> 00:42:22,500
which allow the driver to move the truck in the tunnel, even with smoke.

509
00:42:28,140 --> 00:42:30,820
The trucks are equipped with a hydraulic lift.

510
00:42:33,240 --> 00:42:39,200
They can be raised off the ground and rotated 360 degrees for maximum fire coverage.

511
00:42:43,020 --> 00:42:49,540
And now, the firefighters are able to stay on the scene longer.

512
00:42:53,620 --> 00:42:57,440
We have new long-duration respiration systems,

513
00:42:57,640 --> 00:43:00,480
which can be put on during the ride to the incident.

514
00:43:01,700 --> 00:43:05,680
And the driver and co-pilots can have respiratory protection

515
00:43:05,681 --> 00:43:08,960
from built-in supplies within the cab as well.

516
00:43:08,961 --> 00:43:15,440
The Goddard Road Tunnel is the longest of the more than two dozen alpine tunnels across Europe.

517
00:43:16,040 --> 00:43:17,600
It's also the most heavily traveled.

518
00:43:18,180 --> 00:43:20,600
About six million vehicles pass through it each year.

519
00:43:20,601 --> 00:43:27,800
When the Goddard Tunnel was planned, the design traffic volume a year was about two million vehicles.

520
00:43:28,980 --> 00:43:32,840
And now we have three times as much vehicles.

521
00:43:33,380 --> 00:43:37,100
So the tunnel is very overstressed by traffic.

522
00:43:37,101 --> 00:43:42,020
and this is mainly due to the increasing merchant traffic.

523
00:43:43,460 --> 00:43:47,160
To cut down on the number of trucks and hazardous material loads in the road tunnels,

524
00:43:47,780 --> 00:43:52,120
the Alp Transit Authority of Switzerland has started construction of a new rail tunnel system.

525
00:43:53,800 --> 00:43:59,340
The will of Swiss people today is to decrease the truck traffic

526
00:43:59,341 --> 00:44:03,060
and to put all these merchandises on rail.

527
00:44:03,061 --> 00:44:11,060
The new Transalpine Railroad will carry both passengers and cargo, particularly heavy goods and hazardous materials.

528
00:44:11,061 --> 00:44:16,135
And this rail tunnel is able to transport 300 trains a day.

529
00:44:16,136 --> 00:44:19,060
There are trains about 1,000 tons.

530
00:44:19,061 --> 00:44:28,060
We'll have less trucks on our highways, they'll be less congested, and safety in the Gotthard Road Tunnel, for example, will increase by a lot.

531
00:44:28,061 --> 00:44:32,060
The massive project will be completed in 2015.

532
00:44:32,061 --> 00:44:39,500
So until then, the traffic in the Goddard Road Tunnel will continue to increase every year.

533
00:44:39,501 --> 00:44:43,980
But with an improved ventilation system and new safety equipment, the Goddard Tunnel has

534
00:44:43,981 --> 00:44:54,620
taken significant steps to minimize some of the risk for future disasters.

